    <!-- ── How to use ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="how-to-use" class="docs-section-title">How to use this page</h2>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        You have a component library and a Storybook, and you want an agent to
        use your components instead of inventing props or writing raw HTML.
        Storybook’s MCP gives it three tools: <code>docs-list</code> (ids and a
        one-line summary), <code>docs-show</code> (one component: description,
        inputs, the first stories with code) and <code>docs-show-story</code>
        (one story). They print what the build’s manifest holds. If a fact is
        not in the manifest, the agent does not have it.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        Pick <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)">one component</strong>
        of your own, call <code>docs-show</code> on it, and read the answer as
        if you were the agent. Work through Part A against what you read, then
        skim Part B for the setup. There is no score, only a list to sort.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        Everything here only reads. Three ways to see what the agent sees, from
        most to least convenient: the MCP tools in your agent; the CLI
        (<code
          >npx storybook tools --config-dir &lt;dir&gt; --json docs list</code
        >, no running Storybook needed); and the manifest report
        <code>components.html</code> (check S3). The self-check prompt at the
        end asks the agent to run all of Part A in one go.
      </p>
      <p class="docs-step-desc">
        <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
          >Sort what you find by how much it hurts.</strong
        >
        The labels are the ones from the Figma checklist. <em>Cosmetic</em>
        faults can wait. <em>Degrading</em> faults leave the agent with fewer
        examples than it needs. <em>Blocks the agent</em> faults leave it
        without the information, so it guesses.
      </p>
      <Callout tone="note" title="Scope of this page">
        <p
          style="margin: 0; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ui-color-text-muted)"
        >
          Checked against Storybook 10.6.0 and Atelier’s Angular library. React
          and Vue fill the same slots through <code>react-docgen</code> and
          <code>vue-component-meta</code>. Only <code>docs-show</code> on
          AtlTable was inspected for them (2026-10-20): neither shows a
          description or usage example there, and the stories shown differ (see
          <a href="/screen-from-storybook" class="docs-link-primary"
            >Screen from Storybook</a
          >). Everything else on this page is stated for Angular, and the checks
          say so where it matters.
        </p>
      </Callout>
    </div>

